@packages/lsp import { SigilLsp }

component SigilVsCodeExtension {
  goal {
    Provide a VS Code authoring and review surface for file-backed Sigil workspaces.

    Connect editor-native features to the bundled language server without duplicating deterministic Sigil semantics.
  }

  interface {
    ExtensionPackage {
      provides an installable VSIX whose artifact version is owned by package.json

      the VSIX is distributed through a versioned GitHub Release
    }

    EditorLanguageSupport {
      registers the sigil language for .sigil files

      provides TextMate syntax highlighting before extension-host activation

      starts the bundled language server through vscode-languageclient

      surfaces diagnostics, document symbols, definition navigation, hover, and semantic highlighting

      accepts the bundled server's dynamic registration for supported ownership implementation-source changes
    }

    ComponentPreview {
      contributes the Sigil: Show Component Preview command

      opens the selected component contract and collected expansions as read-only Markdown
    }

    ConfigurationSchema {
      contributes JSON Schema validation and completion for .sigil/config.json using the bundled Sigil configuration schema
    }

    SupportedExtensionHosts {
      runs as a workspace extension in trusted desktop and remote Node extension hosts

      supports file-backed workspaces and file URIs

      does not support VS Code for the Web or virtual workspaces
    }

    ReadOnlyEditorSupport {
      collects no telemetry

      does not modify Sigil documents
    }
  }
}

expand SigilVsCodeExtension {
  state {
    EditorLanguageSupport {
      The language client lifecycle is stopped, starting, running, or stopping.
    }

    ComponentPreview {
      Preview selection reflects the active Sigil editor and cursor when the command runs.
    }
  }

  logic {
    EditorLanguageSupport {
      Opening or activating a Sigil document starts the bundled server when it is not already running.

      The language client forwards document lifecycle and language-feature requests through LSP.

      The language client delivers file events for watcher patterns dynamically registered by the bundled server.

      Startup and runtime failures are written to the Sigil output channel and surfaced to the user.

      Deactivation stops the language client and server process.
    }

    ComponentPreview {
      The preview command requests the standard hover result at the active cursor.

      A successful result opens read-only Markdown beside the Sigil document.

      A missing component or unavailable hover result shows a non-destructive informational message.
    }

    ConfigurationSchema {
      The extension maps .sigil/config.json to the bundled schema through VS Code's standard jsonValidation contribution, and VS Code performs completion and validation.
    }
  }

  constraints {
    ArtifactVersionOwnership {
      package.json owns the extension artifact version.

      The extension Sigil contract does not duplicate its manifest-owned version literal.
    }

    ExtensionPackage {
      The extension bundles its client and Node-compatible server artifacts.

      Users require neither a globally installed Deno runtime nor a Sigil CLI.

      Distribution uses GitHub-hosted VSIX assets rather than Visual Studio Marketplace or Open VSX.
    }

    EditorLanguageSupport {
      The extension uses stable public VS Code APIs and vscode-languageclient.

      The extension depends on SigilLsp for language semantics and does not import or duplicate core behavior.

      Component preview uses standard hover and introduces no custom protocol method.

      Ownership file watching is notification-only and does not modify workspace files.
    }

    ConfigurationSchema {
      The bundled schema is a synchronized copy of spec/sigil-config.schema.json representing the SigilWorkspaceConfig contract without extending it.

      The schema ships inside the VSIX and loads without network access; VS Code's standard JSON language service performs validation and duplicates neither the Sigil LSP nor sigil check.
    }

    SupportedExtensionHosts {
      extensionKind is workspace.

      Extension activation requires a trusted workspace under the current manifest behavior.

      File-backed desktop and remote Node hosts are supported; web and virtual hosts are not.
    }

    ReadOnlyEditorSupport {
      The extension registers no document-editing, file-mutation, or telemetry behavior.
    }
  }

  decisions {
    EditorLanguageSupport {
      Decision: Bundle the Node-compatible Sigil language server and use standard LSP features, including hover-backed component preview, without custom protocol methods.

      Scope: Governs the VS Code client-server integration and component preview; excludes Sigil language interpretation.

      Trade-offs: Bundling increases package size and requires rebuilding the server with the extension.

      Consequences: Preview, navigation, diagnostics, and highlighting remain compatible with the shared LSP contract.

      Revisit when: A separately installed shared server becomes operationally preferable.
    }

    ConfigurationSchema {
      Decision: Provide .sigil/config.json authoring support by bundling the published spec/sigil-config.schema.json and associating it through VS Code's standard jsonValidation contribution, rather than adding configuration validation to the Sigil LSP.

      Scope: Governs .sigil/config.json editor authoring support; excludes the Sigil language, the configuration format, the CLI, and LSP behavior.

      Design issues addressed: Contributors editing configuration get no schema-backed help, while the contract already exists as a published schema; duplicating it in the LSP would fork the contract.

      Consequences: The schema is a synchronized copy shipped in the VSIX and loaded offline; VS Code performs completion and validation; a stale copy is caught by a synchronization test and refreshed at build time.

      Revisit when: Configuration authoring needs editor behavior beyond standard JSON validation.
    }

    OwnershipCacheInvalidation {
      Decision: Let the bundled language server dynamically register supported ownership-source watcher patterns through standard Language Server Protocol capability registration.

      Scope: Governs VS Code delivery of registered ownership-source changes; excludes ownership parsing, cache storage, and source-support policy.

      Trade-offs: Server-directed registration removes the redundant extension-owned watcher while depending on the language client's dynamic-registration support.

      Consequences: The extension does not duplicate supported source patterns and the server receives standard workspace/didChangeWatchedFiles notifications.

      Revisit when: The language client cannot reliably honor dynamic registration or the protocol provides incremental ownership-index updates.
    }

    SupportedExtensionHosts {
      Decision: Limit the initial extension to trusted file-backed desktop and remote Node workspace hosts.

      Scope: Governs the pre-production extension-host support contract; excludes future browser and virtual-workspace adapters.

      Trade-offs: Restricted, virtual, and browser workspaces receive no full extension support.

      Consequences: The manifest explicitly declares workspace placement and unsupported trust or virtual-workspace modes.

      Revisit when: The server supports browser or VS Code filesystem abstractions, or a security review approves a useful Restricted Mode subset.
    }
  }

  cases {
    EditorLanguageSupport {
      Opening a .sigil file applies TextMate highlighting and starts language features.

      Malformed source displays ranged diagnostics.

      Imported components and concepts support navigation, hover, and semantic highlighting.

      Changing an ownership annotation invalidates the server cache and the next component or concept hover shows refreshed links.

      A failed bundled server produces visible output and an error without crashing VS Code.

      Deactivation stops the client and server.
    }

    ComponentPreview {
      Running preview on a component opens read-only Markdown.

      Running preview without an active component leaves editors unchanged and shows information.
    }

    ConfigurationSchema {
      Opening a valid .sigil/config.json provides schema-backed completion without diagnostics.

      An unknown configuration property or invalid value shape is reported by VS Code JSON validation.
    }

    SupportedExtensionHosts {
      A trusted desktop or remote file-backed workspace receives full language support.

      Restricted Mode disables the extension under current manifest defaults.

      A virtual or web workspace receives no supported extension-host contract.
    }

    ExtensionPackage {
      A user installs the VSIX downloaded from GitHub Releases through Install from VSIX.
    }
  }
}
